The most common reason is that the **parent re-renders**, which re-renders all its children by default in React — even if the props haven't changed. React doesn't skip child renders just because props are the same.

To fix it, wrap your child component in `React.memo()`:

```javascript
const Child = React.memo(({ prop1, prop2 }) => {
  return <div>{prop1} {prop2}</div>;
});
```

Now the child only re-renders if its props actually change.

**But watch for this:** if you pass objects, arrays, or functions as props, they're recreated on every render. React sees them as new and re-renders anyway. Example of the problem:

```javascript
// Bad — style is a new object each time
<Child style={{ color: 'red' }} />

// Good — style is stable
const style = { color: 'red' };
<Child style={style} />
```

Use `useMemo()` or `useCallback()` to keep props stable if they come from calculations or functions. That's usually the real culprit when `React.memo()` doesn't seem to help.
